Z||000004||88 Fiero||04-11-2000||01:29 AM||||MW...according to Fiero Doc you can install an Eibach Front Spring on an '88 Fiero GT. You see....Bob Yorks 3.4 Intercooled Turbo Fiero got those Eibachs Front Springs...But this spring is from the '84 to '87. he said that the configuration is the same as the '88 but you need to cut the coil because the Eibach's are longer that the '88 stock Fiero. I will be ordering the Eibach's Fronts and will be installing it on my '88. When Fiero Doc gave me a ride on Bob Yorks car it handle pretty well...I think you can also used the ST's springs too.....||||reg||1
Z||000005||phillip||04-11-2000||01:36 AM||||i ordered some from jc whitney about a month ago (susp. tech) and just got a notice that they were back ordered another month. it's the best price though, about $85 so i guess i'll wait.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mwbackus||04-11-2000||05:51 AM||||88 Fiero:<P>I had not heard of this before, and I am a bit confused as to what you are referring to.  Are you saying that you are going to buy a new set of 84 - 87 lowering springs and cut them so they will fit an 88?  If that is the case, then why not just cut the factory springs?  It would offer the same (if not better) result.. and be much cheaper.<P>Mike B.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000007||Eric||04-11-2000||10:23 AM||||I think the eibachs are progressive rate, so cutting a coil would make the eibach spring match the stock coil before it's on the car.  Once the car is on the ground, the suspension should "relax" and settle into a position that is approximatly 1" lower.  Cutting the stock springs is not a better solution if this method works.<P>88 Fiero, did Fiero Doc's car have stock rear springs?  Keep us updated.||||reg||1
Z||000008||88 Fiero||04-11-2000||11:10 AM||||Eric...Fiero Doc said that the rears are not going to work...MW...when he gave me a ride on Bob Yorks '88 GT...it was fine..he even run it on a railroad tracks and it's not that bad. It didnt even rattle my teeth or even launch up in the air. I know that some stiff springs will make you wish your driving in a flat surface...The good part about this is that it's firmer and better than stock.. My '88's front springs are cut and I also cut down my bumpstops to avoid bottoming out...The factory springs for some reason is still a little soft than the Eibach ones. I will still go ahead order the Eibach's Front Springs. Phillip...why don't you contact Suspension Technics themselves and see if you can buy it thru them directly....||||reg||1
Z||000009||87PegasusGT||04-11-2000||01:22 PM||||Do you have to replace shocks and struts with lowering springs? Or can I install lowering springs now and do the others later?||||reg||1
Z||000010||mwbackus||04-11-2000||01:26 PM||||According to my information and the experience of others, rear lowering springs will work regardless of the year intended (same spring design).  The best solution is to just buy the "correct" front lowering springs for your car.  Cutting up a pair of $150 springs is not something most would consider, regardless of how well it might work.<P>Mike B.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Eric||04-12-2000||09:21 AM||||I'll let 88 Fiero try this method first.  88 Fiero, did you mean that the stock rear springs won't work with the lowered front springs?||||reg||1
